# Whatever env vars the configured model provider needs, credentials
# included. Copy to provider.env, which is not in git:
#
#     cp configs/env/provider.env.example configs/env/provider.env
#
# Read only by the three services that call a model: extraction, topic
# modelling and question generation. The api is given the model settings and
# never these, because it sends no request to the model.
#
# litellm reads whichever its own provider needs, so nothing here is named by
# this project. Leave the file empty for a plain Ollama, which needs none of
# it. Which provider is used is not set here at all - it is the prefix of
# LLM_MODEL in .env.
#
#     LLM_MODEL=ollama_chat/gemma4:31b       a local Ollama, no key
#     LLM_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o                OPENAI_API_KEY
#     LLM_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#     LLM_MODEL=gemini/gemini-2.5-pro        GEMINI_API_KEY
#     LLM_MODEL=hosted_vllm/<model>          HOSTED_VLLM_API_KEY
#     LLM_MODEL=bedrock/<model>              AWS_* below
#     LLM_MODEL=azure/<deployment>           AZURE_* below
#
# `make doctor` asks the configured model one question and reports what it
# answered, which is where a wrong key or an unreachable address surfaces.

# ── A hosted provider with one key ────────────────────────────────────────
# Uncomment the one that matches LLM_MODEL's prefix.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# HOSTED_VLLM_API_KEY=

# ── Ollama behind a gateway ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# A plain Ollama needs none of this.
# OLLAMA_API_KEY=

# ── Bedrock ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_REGION_NAME=

# ── Azure ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# With LLM_MODEL=azure/<deployment>, where the deployment name is the one in
# the Foundry resource rather than the model's own name.
AZURE_API_VERSION=2024-12-01-preview
# AZURE_API_BASE=https://<resource>.openai.azure.com

# A key, if that is how this deployment authenticates.
# AZURE_API_KEY=

# ── Entra ID instead of a key, on an azure/* model ────────────────────────
#
# Needs no key variable here, and which one works depends on WHERE the
# process runs. A second factor is asked for when a token is issued, not
# when it is used, so a container never satisfies one itself: it is given
# an identity that has none, or lent the result of a login somebody else
# already did.
#
# A service principal is the one a container can use on its own. Creating
# it needs directory rights the user running the pipeline may not have.
# AZURE_TENANT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# With none of the three set, DefaultAzureCredential is tried: managed or
# workload identity on Azure infrastructure, then `az login` for a host-side
# run. A container reaches neither unless it is given both a mounted
# ~/.azure and an az binary the image does not carry, which is the usual
# reason LLM_CONTAINER_MODEL names a local model instead.
#
# A scope, only if the endpoint wants something other than
# https://cognitiveservices.azure.com/.default
# AZURE_SCOPE=
